WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:04.470
-Welcome, welcome, welcome,
welcome to "The Tonight Show"!

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:06.806
[ Cheers and applause ]
Come on.

00:00:06.806 --> 00:00:08.307
Look how festive it looks.

00:00:08.307 --> 00:00:10.510
Guys, let's get
to the news and jokes.

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:13.546
Well, guys, this is exciting
and historic day,

00:00:13.546 --> 00:00:15.615
because the United Kingdom
started giving out

00:00:15.615 --> 00:00:18.818
the first doses of Pfizer's
COVID vaccine.

00:00:18.818 --> 00:00:19.852
Watch this.

00:00:19.852 --> 00:00:22.555
-At 6:31 this morning,
Margaret Keenan,

00:00:22.555 --> 00:00:24.490
who turns 91 next week,

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:27.427
became the first person
ever given a tested

00:00:27.427 --> 00:00:30.763
and vetted COVID vaccine
outside of trials.

00:00:30.763 --> 00:00:33.099
-That's right, 90-year-old
Margaret Keenan

00:00:33.099 --> 00:00:35.134
is the first patient
in the world

00:00:35.134 --> 00:00:37.904
to receive the Pfizer vaccine.

00:00:37.904 --> 00:00:41.373
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:41.373 --> 00:00:44.777
Or as the Queen put it,
"Sure, sure, she was."

00:00:44.777 --> 00:00:46.145
[ Laughter ]

00:00:46.145 --> 00:00:50.382
"Yes, we definitely gave it
to Margaret Keenan first."

00:00:50.382 --> 00:00:52.985
The U.K. vaccine underwent
rigorous testing.

00:00:52.985 --> 00:00:55.154
Apparently Gordon Ramsay
would try every version

00:00:55.154 --> 00:00:56.756
and then berate the scientist.

00:00:56.756 --> 00:00:59.392
"You donkey, it's not a vaccine!
It's raw!"

00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:00.593
[ Laughter ]

00:01:00.593 --> 00:01:03.429
"It's meant to be
kept in the freezer, donkey!"

00:01:03.429 --> 00:01:05.998
[ Laughter ]

00:01:05.998 --> 00:01:08.600
Yeah, the vaccine
is starting in the U.K.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:10.336
and then coming here.

00:01:10.336 --> 00:01:12.038
But your one annoying friend
will be like,

00:01:12.038 --> 00:01:14.841
"I actually liked the original
British version better."

00:01:14.841 --> 00:01:16.276
[ Laughter ]

00:01:16.276 --> 00:01:18.745
"Just nuanced
and way more subtle."

00:01:18.745 --> 00:01:20.079
[ Laughter ]

00:01:20.079 --> 00:01:22.048
That's right, it's a big day
over in the U.K.

00:01:22.048 --> 00:01:25.351
People are calling it "V-Day"
for Vaccination Day.

00:01:25.351 --> 00:01:27.754
Yeah, V-Day, not to be
confused with tonight's

00:01:27.754 --> 00:01:29.655
"Bachelorette" episode "VD Day."

00:01:29.655 --> 00:01:31.124
That's a different --
[ Laughter ]

00:01:31.124 --> 00:01:32.659
Different thing.

00:01:32.659 --> 00:01:34.994
Meanwhile, this is going viral.
It turns out --

00:01:34.994 --> 00:01:38.264
this is real -- the second
person to get the vaccine

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:42.335
was an 81-year-old man
named William Shakespeare.

00:01:42.335 --> 00:01:44.670
[ Laughter ]

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:48.074
Yeah, William Shakespeare
got a vaccine on V-Day.

00:01:48.074 --> 00:01:49.676
Which explains why he said,

00:01:49.676 --> 00:01:55.548
"To V or not to V,
that is the injection."

00:01:55.548 --> 00:01:57.083
[ Laughter ]

00:01:57.083 --> 00:01:59.719
"'Tis nobler to take Moderna."

00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:02.321
[ Laughter ]

00:02:02.321 --> 00:02:04.957
I'm glad Shakespeare got
the vaccine because in 81 years

00:02:04.957 --> 00:02:07.760
he hasn't been able to get
a dinner reservation.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:10.029
"Yes, the name's
William Shakespeare.

00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:12.031
Hello?"
[ Laughter ]

00:02:12.031 --> 00:02:14.801
"They hung up."
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:16.602
[ Laughter ]

00:02:16.602 --> 00:02:18.638
-That one got you, man?
-Yeah.

00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:20.340
-That's funny.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.141
Imagine living in the U.K.
and being named

00:02:22.141 --> 00:02:24.209
after their greatest writer,
William Shakespeare.

00:02:24.209 --> 00:02:26.146
That's like living in the U.S.
and being named after

00:02:26.146 --> 00:02:27.814
whoever writes
the Geico commercials.

00:02:27.814 --> 00:02:29.081
[ Laughter ]

00:02:29.081 --> 00:02:31.251
They're geniuses.

00:02:31.251 --> 00:02:33.119
I don't know what's
in the vaccine,

00:02:33.119 --> 00:02:35.855
but it must be pretty amazing,
'cause after Shakespeare got it,

00:02:35.855 --> 00:02:37.991
he challenged Logan Paul
to a boxing match.

00:02:37.991 --> 00:02:41.894
[ Laughter ]

00:02:41.894 --> 00:02:44.130
Well, as the U.K.
starts vaccinating,

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:46.232
some news just broke out
about our own supply.

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:49.268
It turns out this summer the
Trump administration turned down

00:02:49.268 --> 00:02:53.105
Pfizer when they asked
if we wanted to buy more doses.

00:02:53.105 --> 00:02:54.207
Trump knows he messed up.

00:02:54.207 --> 00:02:55.541
When asked about it,
he was like,

00:02:55.541 --> 00:02:57.843
"I'm only answering questions
about Mario Lopez

00:02:57.843 --> 00:02:59.112
playing Colonel Sanders."

00:02:59.112 --> 00:03:01.547
[ Laughter ]

00:03:01.547 --> 00:03:02.782
Speaking of the White House,

00:03:02.782 --> 00:03:05.652
I read that Trump
wants to hand out mass pardons

00:03:05.652 --> 00:03:08.855
to "every person
who ever talked to him,

00:03:08.855 --> 00:03:11.891
even people who
don't need pardons."

00:03:11.891 --> 00:03:14.193
Yep, Trump's handing out
mass pardons.

00:03:14.193 --> 00:03:15.861
The only other time you hear
that phrase

00:03:15.861 --> 00:03:18.397
is after Rudy Giuliani
eats Taco Bell.

00:03:18.397 --> 00:03:20.199
"Pardon -- Pardon me."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:20.199 --> 00:03:21.400
"Oop, pardon.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.502
Pardon me, sorry.

00:03:23.502 --> 00:03:24.937
Oh, that's a half pardon,
sorry."

00:03:24.937 --> 00:03:26.939
[ Laughter ]

00:03:26.939 --> 00:03:28.340
Oh, and of course,
President Trump

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:30.876
is still refusing to accept
the results of the election,

00:03:30.876 --> 00:03:32.245
but he's not the only one.

00:03:32.245 --> 00:03:33.846
A new "Washington Post"
survey says

00:03:33.846 --> 00:03:36.782
89% of all Republicans
serving in Congress

00:03:36.782 --> 00:03:38.684
won't accept Biden's victory.

00:03:38.684 --> 00:03:40.152
Here's what a few Republicans
said

00:03:40.152 --> 00:03:41.587
when asked about the election.

00:03:41.587 --> 00:03:44.757 line:0%
First up,
Congressman Greg Pence said,

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:47.827 line:0%
"If Biden wins, I'll quit,
and focus on my Etsy product,

00:03:47.827 --> 00:03:49.295 line:0%
Lipstick for Men."

00:03:49.295 --> 00:03:52.664 line:0%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:52.664 --> 00:03:55.234 line:0%
Up next,
Senator Tom Cotton said,

00:03:55.234 --> 00:03:57.536 line:0%
"Trump will be president
by the time that gumball

00:03:57.536 --> 00:04:00.306 line:0%
I swallowed last year finally
works its way down my throat."

00:04:00.306 --> 00:04:02.374 line:0%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:02.374 --> 00:04:04.744 line:0%
Next up, Congressman
Dusty Johnson

00:04:04.744 --> 00:04:06.012 line:0%
was like,
"Don't bother me.

00:04:06.012 --> 00:04:07.480 line:0%
I'm busy losing a chess match

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:09.415 line:0%
to that girl
from 'Queen's Gambit.'"

00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:10.750 line:0%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:13.485 line:0%
Up next, Congressman
Fred Upton said,

00:04:13.485 --> 00:04:15.654 line:0%
"I trust the results,
but not the congressmen

00:04:15.654 --> 00:04:18.124 line:0%
who told me 'everyone'
was doing this pose."

00:04:18.124 --> 00:04:22.261 line:0%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:22.261 --> 00:04:24.130 line:0%
Then, Senator Mike Braun
was like,

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:26.865 line:0%
"This election was rigged
or I wasn't once an extra

00:04:26.865 --> 00:04:28.968 line:0%
in a 'Sopranos'
restaurant scene."

00:04:28.968 --> 00:04:30.703 line:0%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:30.703 --> 00:04:32.905 line:0%
And finally,
Senator John Boozman said,

00:04:32.905 --> 00:04:35.508 line:0%
"If you have proof the election
was rigged, I'm all ears."

00:04:35.508 --> 00:04:40.212 line:0%
[ Groaning, laughter, applause ]

00:04:40.212 --> 00:04:41.814
Very interesting stuff.

00:04:41.814 --> 00:04:43.249
Here's a big tech story.

00:04:43.249 --> 00:04:46.319
Today, Apple unveiled a pair
of noise-canceling headphones

00:04:46.319 --> 00:04:48.253
called the AirPods Max.

00:04:48.253 --> 00:04:51.557
And get this -- they cost $549.

00:04:51.557 --> 00:04:53.826
Check them out. Yep.

00:04:53.826 --> 00:04:56.963
Noise-canceling headphones
for $549.

00:04:56.963 --> 00:04:58.898
When they heard,
people quarantining with kids

00:04:58.898 --> 00:05:00.466
were like,
"I'll give you $800."

00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:02.601
[ Laughter ]

00:05:02.601 --> 00:05:05.571
Here are the headphones
in their case.

00:05:05.571 --> 00:05:07.707
Yeah, great. It looks like
a stormtrooper's purse.

00:05:07.707 --> 00:05:09.208
[ Laughter ]

00:05:09.208 --> 00:05:10.609
Hey, listen to this.

00:05:10.609 --> 00:05:11.878
I saw that Pizza Hut is

00:05:11.878 --> 00:05:13.779
selling something new
for the holidays.

00:05:13.779 --> 00:05:15.047
Check this out.

00:05:15.047 --> 00:05:17.549
It's the new triple-decker box
from Pizza Hut.

00:05:17.549 --> 00:05:20.253
Look at this thing.

00:05:20.253 --> 00:05:22.522
In America, that's actually
our food pyramid.

00:05:22.522 --> 00:05:24.357
[ Laughter ]
That's right.

00:05:24.357 --> 00:05:26.558
In the U.K.,
they have a COVID vaccine.

00:05:26.558 --> 00:05:29.262
In America, we came up with
the triple-decker pizza box.

00:05:29.262 --> 00:05:30.429
[ Laughter ]

00:05:30.429 --> 00:05:31.964
It's a great idea.

00:05:31.964 --> 00:05:33.565
My biggest complaint
about ordering pizza

00:05:33.565 --> 00:05:35.667
was always not having
enough cardboard

00:05:35.667 --> 00:05:38.304
to awkwardly fold and smoosh
down in my garbage can.

00:05:38.304 --> 00:05:40.139
[ Laughter ]

00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:41.941
I'm turning into
Andy Rooney now.

00:05:41.941 --> 00:05:43.275
[ Laughter ]

00:05:43.275 --> 00:05:48.114
"That's the part I need
more of when I get a pizza.

00:05:48.114 --> 00:05:52.118
Having to fold the cardboard
and put it in my garbage can."

00:05:52.118 --> 00:05:55.388
[ Laughter ]

00:05:55.388 --> 00:05:57.823
I can almost picture idiot guys
across the country going,

00:05:57.823 --> 00:06:00.193
"Honey, you could use this
as a jewelry box."

00:06:00.193 --> 00:06:02.494
[ Laughter ]

00:06:02.494 --> 00:06:04.263
"Just saying."

00:06:04.263 --> 00:06:06.231
I saw that after 70 years,

00:06:06.231 --> 00:06:08.467
IKEA is ending
its popular catalog.

00:06:08.467 --> 00:06:09.569
[ Crowd awws ]

00:06:09.569 --> 00:06:10.903
I know.
At its peak,

00:06:10.903 --> 00:06:13.806
IKEA printed up to 200 million
copies in one year,

00:06:13.806 --> 00:06:16.976
making it one of the most
popular books in the world.

00:06:16.976 --> 00:06:18.811
So we put together
this little tribute

00:06:18.811 --> 00:06:21.781
to our favorite products
in the catalog over the years.

00:06:21.781 --> 00:06:22.715
Enjoy.

00:06:22.715 --> 00:06:32.358 line:0%
♪♪

00:06:32.358 --> 00:06:41.967 line:0%
♪♪

00:06:41.967 --> 00:06:45.604
[ Dial-up modem ringing ]

00:06:45.604 --> 00:06:48.674 line:0%
♪♪

00:06:48.674 --> 00:06:50.743
[ Applause ]

00:06:50.743 --> 00:06:52.678
Here's a wild story.

00:06:52.678 --> 00:06:55.481
Two cousins here
in New York City ages 7 and 12,

00:06:55.481 --> 00:06:58.351
took one of their parents'
Range Rovers for a joy ride

00:06:58.351 --> 00:07:00.986
and ended up at a rest stop
near Delaware.

00:07:00.986 --> 00:07:02.621
The 12-year-old was like,
"Don't blame me.

00:07:02.621 --> 00:07:03.990
He was the one driving."

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:05.658
[ Laughter ]

00:07:05.658 --> 00:07:07.526
It got worse when the kids
passed the officer

00:07:07.526 --> 00:07:09.127
a Chuck E. Cheese token
and said,

00:07:09.127 --> 00:07:11.030
"What do you say
we make this thing go away?"

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:13.599
[ Laughter ]

00:07:13.599 --> 00:07:16.068
And finally,
I heard about a man in Italy

00:07:16.068 --> 00:07:19.872
who walked 280 miles
after a fight with his wife,

00:07:19.872 --> 00:07:22.909
then got fined
for violating lockdown rules.

00:07:22.909 --> 00:07:24.109
[ Laughter ]

00:07:24.109 --> 00:07:25.645
I'm no marriage counselor,

00:07:25.645 --> 00:07:26.878
but it's probably
not a great sign

00:07:26.878 --> 00:07:29.115
when someone storms out
and walks to Switzerland.

00:07:29.115 --> 00:07:30.382
[ Laughter ]

00:07:30.382 --> 00:07:32.217
That's last time
he gets his wife

00:07:32.217 --> 00:07:33.786
a triple-decker pizza box
for their anniversary.

00:07:33.786 --> 00:07:34.987
[ Laughter ]

00:07:34.987 --> 00:07:38.490
Guys, we have
exactly nine shows left

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:41.059
before we go on Christmas break,
which means it's time

00:07:41.059 --> 00:07:42.961
for that beloved
"Tonight Show" tradition.

00:07:42.961 --> 00:07:47.332
It's time for "12 Days
of Christmas Sweaters."

00:07:47.332 --> 00:07:53.372
♪ -12 days of Christmas
sweaters, 9 days left ♪

00:07:53.372 --> 00:07:54.607
-That's right.

00:07:54.607 --> 00:07:56.609
Every show between now
and Christmas,

00:07:56.609 --> 00:07:59.478
we'll be giving
one lucky audience member

00:07:59.478 --> 00:08:01.781
a stunning Christmas sweater

00:08:01.781 --> 00:08:03.416
from the "Countdown to
Christmas Cabinet."

00:08:03.416 --> 00:08:05.518
So, since there are nine
shows left,

00:08:05.518 --> 00:08:08.554
let's open door number nine.

00:08:08.554 --> 00:08:10.489
[ Drumroll ]
It's right there.

00:08:10.489 --> 00:08:12.291
Very exciting. Yes.

00:08:12.291 --> 00:08:15.093
Whenever you're ready. Yes.

00:08:15.093 --> 00:08:16.829
Yes! Someone said -- Whoa!

00:08:16.829 --> 00:08:19.231
That's a lot of --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:19.231 --> 00:08:23.168
That's a lot of stuff.

00:08:23.168 --> 00:08:27.306
Oh, good -- good gosh almighty.

00:08:27.306 --> 00:08:28.474
[ Laughter ]

00:08:28.474 --> 00:08:30.108
That's a lot of sweater
that was in there.

00:08:30.108 --> 00:08:31.243
Oh, my goodness gracious.

00:08:31.243 --> 00:08:32.645
Is there something on the back?

00:08:32.645 --> 00:08:33.913
Oh, my gosh!

00:08:33.913 --> 00:08:35.680
Well, there's a little man
hidden on there.

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:37.049
There's a little man.

00:08:37.049 --> 00:08:39.518
A stowaway on the back of that.

00:08:39.518 --> 00:08:42.588
Alright, who is excited
to take home this sweater?

00:08:42.588 --> 00:08:43.822
[ Cheers and applause ]
There we go.

00:08:43.822 --> 00:08:46.158
Alright, if I call your number,
come on down.

00:08:46.158 --> 00:08:48.527
Quest, can I get a drumroll,
please?

00:08:48.527 --> 00:08:52.431
[ Drumroll ]

00:08:52.431 --> 00:08:55.134
1-2-3!

00:08:55.134 --> 00:08:58.771 line:0%
♪♪

00:08:58.771 --> 00:09:00.573
Ah, here he comes.

00:09:00.573 --> 00:09:03.175
Welcome.
Oh, my goodness.

00:09:03.175 --> 00:09:05.878
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:09:05.878 --> 00:09:07.712
What is your name
and where are you from?

00:09:07.712 --> 00:09:10.482
-My name is Tom Russell
from New Rochelle, New York.

00:09:10.482 --> 00:09:11.483
-Hey, New Rochelle.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:11.483 --> 00:09:13.319
I know New Rochelle very well.

00:09:13.319 --> 00:09:14.653
-There's a lot going on here.

00:09:14.653 --> 00:09:17.122
-Well, you know, they say
during the holiday season,

00:09:17.122 --> 00:09:18.324
"make lemonade,"
you know what I'm saying?

00:09:18.324 --> 00:09:20.926
-Mm-hmm.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:20.926 --> 00:09:22.694
-Would you do us
the honor of trying it on?

00:09:22.694 --> 00:09:24.596
-Yeah, which one's the front?
[ Laughs ]

00:09:24.596 --> 00:09:25.964
-That's up to you, buddy.

00:09:25.964 --> 00:09:27.099
It's --
-Alright.

00:09:27.099 --> 00:09:28.500
-It's really that much fun.

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:31.937
You don't know what the front
or the back is, really.

00:09:31.937 --> 00:09:34.440
You know that it has sleeves.

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:36.909
That's -- The only thing we can
guarantee with our sweaters,

00:09:36.909 --> 00:09:39.712
that there are
sometimes sleeves.

00:09:39.712 --> 00:09:42.815
[ Laughter ]
That's a -- oh, I feel like --

00:09:42.815 --> 00:09:45.250
oh, I feel like this
is the right way to go.

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:46.686
[ Laughs ]
Oh, my God!

00:09:46.686 --> 00:09:49.355
Wow! Oh, my God.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:49.355 --> 00:09:50.990
Wow, I don't even know
what to think.

00:09:50.990 --> 00:09:54.193
I'm looking at it and I still
don't know what's happening.

00:09:54.193 --> 00:09:56.062
-I can't see my shoes.
-It's unbelievable.

00:09:56.062 --> 00:09:59.064
This is unbelievable.
It is a holiday miracle.

00:09:59.064 --> 00:10:01.100
Give it up for our winner
right here.

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:02.234
A round of applause.
[ Applause ]

00:10:02.234 --> 00:10:03.635
Stick around.

00:10:03.635 --> 00:10:05.404
We'll be right back with more
"Tonight Show," everybody.

00:10:05.404 --> 00:10:06.772
Come on back.

00:10:06.772 --> 00:10:15.414 line:0%
♪♪

